{"qid":"Q945","names":["Togo"],"title":"Togo","endonym":{"native":"Togo","lang":"ee"},"capsule":"Togo gained independence from French trusteeship on 27 April 1960, with Sylvanus Olympio as its first president; Olympio was assassinated on 13 January 1963 in the first military overthrow of a sub-Saharan African head of state after independence. Gnassingbé Eyadéma seized power in 1967 and held it until his death in 2005 — one of the longest tenures in modern African history — after which his son Faure Gnassingbé assumed the presidency. In 2024 Togo adopted a new constitution shifting from a presidential to a parliamentary system, with Faure Gnassingbé becoming President of the Council of Ministers while a ceremonial president assumed the formal head-of-state role.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Modern Togo's borders are defined by colonial-era treaties and internationally recognised; territorial extent for this period is precisely mapped without ambiguity.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Togo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Togo"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Sylvanus Olympio","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylvanus_Olympio"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Gnassingbé Eyadéma","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnassingb%C3%A9_Eyad%C3%A9ma"}],"britannica":"place/togo","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Togo","rulers":[{"qid":"Q312667","name":"Sylvanus Epiphanio Olympio","start":1960,"end":1961,"image":"Sylvanus Olympio on March 20, 1962 (cropped).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sylvanus%20Olympio%20on%20March%2020%2C%201962%20(cropped).jpg?width=320","imageLicense"